            <ArticleTitle>Ocular signs and symptoms caused by exposure to sarin gas.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>209-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Ocular signs and symptoms that occurred in people exposed to sarin gas in a subway sarin gas attack were studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Among victims of sarin gas exposure, 96 were treated by us.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Ocular signs and symptoms caused by sarin gas exposure included miosis, conjunctival injection, and ocular pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Ocular signs and symptoms spontaneously resolved between three and 21 days after exposure in most cases. Treatment with 0.5% tropicamide ophthalmic solution was effective in decreasing ocular pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
